% ============================================================================
% veto-override — whether Congress can override each kind of presidential
% veto (adj-facts-stdlib, CIVICS).
% ============================================================================
% "Can Congress override a veto?" has two different answers depending on
% WHICH kind of veto the president used, and that contrast is the whole
% content of this table. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? veto_override(pocket_veto, $S)
%       % cannot_be_overridden_by_congress
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `veto_override(veto_type, override_status)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the status WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on any veto kind not one of these two source-cited
% rows. The query also runs BACKWARD:
%
%     ? veto_override($T, cannot_be_overridden_by_congress)
%       % pocket_veto
%
% THE HEDGE IS PART OF THE FACT. The source does NOT say Congress can
% always override an ordinary veto — it says "in most cases Congress can
% vote to override that veto". Collapsing that to a bare `yes` would state
% something the source deliberately does not, so the atom keeps the
% qualifier: `congress_can_override_in_most_cases`. The pocket-veto row
% carries no such hedge because its own sentence carries none ("it cannot
% be overridden by Congress"), and the asymmetry between the two atoms is
% therefore a faithful reflection of the source rather than sloppy
% parallelism.
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from USA.gov's "How laws are made" page,
% "How a bill becomes a law" section:
%
%     veto_type (this table's atom)  override_status (this table's atom)      quote (verbatim)
%     -----------------------------  ---------------------------------------  ---------------------------------------------------
%     veto                           congress_can_override_in_most_cases       "If the president chooses to veto a bill, in most
%                                                                                 cases Congress can vote to override that veto
%                                                                                 and the bill becomes a law."
%     pocket_veto                    cannot_be_overridden_by_congress          "This action is called a pocket veto, and it
%                                                                                 cannot be overridden by Congress."
%
% curl-fetched the raw page and read it byte-for-byte before writing this
% file (not just an AI-summarized WebFetch). `trust authoritative` —
% USA.gov is the U.S. General Services Administration's official guide to
% government information, a first-party .gov publisher of exactly the fact
% being recalled, the same tier and the same page
% `chamber-exclusive-power.adj` already cites.
%
% NEW AXIS, not a restatement. `checks-and-balances.adj` already ships
% `checks_and_balances(president, veto, legislation_created_by_congress)`
% — that row says the veto EXISTS as an act one branch takes against
% another. This table says something that row cannot: whether the act can
% be UNDONE, and by whom. The two compose rather than overlap — the check
% and its own counter-check. Note `veto` appears in both files as an atom,
% but in different argument positions of different predicates: there it is
% an ACTION, here it is a KIND of veto. Distinct predicates, so there is no
% collision to disambiguate, and a recall against one can never be confused
% with a recall against the other.
%
% Honest abstention:
%
%   - The CONDITIONS that produce a pocket veto — "if the president does
%     not sign off on a bill and it remains unsigned when Congress is no
%     longer in session, the bill will be vetoed by default" — are stated
%     by the same paragraph, but they answer WHEN a pocket veto happens,
%     not whether it can be overridden. That is a different axis and
%     belongs in its own table rather than flattened into this one.
%   - `line_item_veto` is a real term in U.S. civics vocabulary, but this
%     source never names it, so a recall on it ABSTAINS rather than
%     inventing an override status for it.
%   - The president's other two options the same paragraph states —
%     approving and signing a bill into law — are not vetoes at all and
%     have no override status to record.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table veto_override {
    columns veto_type, override_status

    row (veto, congress_can_override_in_most_cases)
    row (pocket_veto, cannot_be_overridden_by_congress)

    source "If the president chooses to veto a bill, in most cases Congress can vote to override that veto and the bill becomes a law."
    locator "https://www.usa.gov/how-laws-are-made"
    trust authoritative

    cites "This action is called a pocket veto, and it cannot be overridden by Congress." locator "https://www.usa.gov/how-laws-are-made"
}
